1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Health Information Act 2026: Legacy Patient Records. A guide for Singapore clinics, pharmacies and hospital groups

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,842
The Health Information Act 2026 covers records going forward.

3
00:00:12,842 --> 00:00:22,094
It does not require historical records to be uploaded to the NEHR, so legacy paper files stay outside the Act but inside the PDPA.

4
00:00:22,094 --> 00:00:28,500
A clinic can be HIA-compliant on new records and still carry a PDPA risk in its storeroom.

5
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:38,511
When: January 2026. What begins: Act passed in Parliament. When: September 2027. What begins: NEHR contribution and data-security duties for covered entities.

6
00:00:38,511 --> 00:00:44,500
When: September 2028. What begins: Reasonable security measures for providers not required to contribute.

7
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:54,311
The HIA requires prospective records to be contributed to the NEHR. Historical records and past consultation notes are not required to be uploaded.

8
00:00:54,311 --> 00:01:04,500
Legacy files remain subject to PDPA retention and protection duties and MOH licensing conditions. Retention commonly runs several years beyond a patient's last treatment.

9
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:15,561
1. Prospective records: contributed to the NEHR under HIA security duties. 2. Legacy paper records: scanned, indexed, then retained or disposed of under the PDPA. 3.

10
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:20,500
Longest-kept records: optional archival microfilm backup alongside digital scans.

11
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:29,753
Must clinics digitise old patient records under the HIA? No. The HIA covers prospective records only. When do the duties start?

12
00:01:29,753 --> 00:01:39,500
September 2027 for covered entities; September 2028 for others. Does the PDPA still apply to old files? Yes. Retention and protection duties continue.

